WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.120
The March 2011 Tohoku earthquake occurred on the subduction interface offshore

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.940
of northeastern Japan. A large rupture displaced the seafloor and generated

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:15.060
a destructive tsunami. The tsunami inundated coastal communities and damaged power

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.940
and cooling systems at Fukushima.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.960
This links a geological event to cascading infrastructure failure.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.880
Aftershocks outline part of the affected fault region and can damage

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.480
weakened structures. A tsunami forecast map shows modeled wave amplitudes, not

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.500
a direct map of earthquake magnitudes.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.780
The 2010 Haiti earthquake occurred near a complex plate-boundary fault system

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.640
with strike-slip and thrust motion.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.540
Severe building collapse caused major losses;

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.800
“near a transform boundary” does not prove that every fault slipped

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.400
purely horizontally. The 2023 Turkey–Syria sequence occurred in a region of

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.460
interacting faults and plates. Major strike-slip ruptures and numerous aftershocks affected

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.940
a densely settled area. Building vulnerability, population exposure, and disrupted services

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.260
help explain losses. A smaller magnitude can cause catastrophic damage when

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.200
the rupture is close to vulnerable communities.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.260
The textbook and lecture use historical estimates of magnitude, deaths, and

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.980
damage. Those estimates should be tied to their source date rather

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.000
than presented as a permanent leaderboard.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.180
The supplied October 27 lecture also compares New Zealand events.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.700
The current textbook section instead emphasizes Turkey–Syria;

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.020
these are different source selections, not grounds to silently rename one

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:34.700
event as another. For comparison practice, identify the tectonic setting, the

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.840
immediate physical hazard, and the factors that turn that hazard into

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:38.220
losses.
